# Runbook - how to set up and run this project

This is the practical guide. The README explains what the project is. This file
explains how to get it running from nothing.

Expect about 45 minutes for the first setup and about 9 minutes for a full
pipeline run after that.

---

## 1. What you need first

| Requirement | Notes |
|---|---|
| Azure subscription | The storage account and access connector live here |
| Azure Databricks workspace | Premium tier, Unity Catalog enabled |
| ADLS Gen2 storage account | Hierarchical namespace turned on |
| Unity Catalog metastore | One per region, attached to the workspace |
| Databricks CLI | Version 0.2xx or newer, for bundle deployment |
| GitHub repository | Only needed for the CI/CD part |

You also need permission to create a catalog, an external location and a storage
credential in Unity Catalog. In a company workspace this is usually a metastore
admin. On your own subscription you already have it.

---

## 2. Azure setup, done once

1. Create a storage account with hierarchical namespace enabled. This project
   uses `stsfactorydev2026`.
2. Create a container inside it. This project uses `smart-factory-dev`.
3. Create an Azure Databricks access connector and give it the
   **Storage Blob Data Contributor** role on the storage account.
4. In Databricks, create a storage credential pointing at that access connector.
   This project uses `sc-smart-factory-dev`.

If you use different names, change them in these files:

- `notebooks/00_setup/01_create_external_locations.py`
- `notebooks/00_setup/02_create_catalog_and_schemas.sql`
- `notebooks/00_setup/03_create_volumes.sql`
- the `CATALOG` value in every `00_config` notebook

---

## 3. Get the code into the workspace

Either clone the repository through **Workspace > Repos > Add repo**, or deploy
the bundle from your machine:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-user>/databricks-smart-factory-data-platform.git
cd databricks-smart-factory-data-platform

export DATABRICKS_HOST=https://adb-xxxxxxxx.azuredatabricks.net
export DATABRICKS_TOKEN=dapi...

databricks bundle validate -t dev
databricks bundle deploy -t dev
```

`databricks.yml` has a variable called `existing_cluster_id`. Change its default
to your own cluster id, or pass it at deploy time:

```bash
databricks bundle deploy -t dev --var="existing_cluster_id=0000-000000-abcdefgh"
```

Use a small all purpose cluster with Databricks Runtime 14.3 LTS or newer.

---

## 4. One time setup notebooks

Run these in order, once. Attach each to your cluster and use **Run all**.

| Order | Notebook | What it creates |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `00_setup/01_create_external_locations.py` | External location `ext_smart_factory_dev` |
| 2 | `00_setup/02_create_catalog_and_schemas.sql` | Catalog `smart_factory_dev` and the schemas `raw`, `bronze`, `silver`, `gold`, `quarantine`, `monitoring` |
| 3 | `00_setup/03_create_volumes.sql` | Volumes `landing_files`, `checkpoint_files`, `archive_files`, `quarantine_files` and the landing folder tree |
| 4 | `07_monitoring_audit/01_create_audit_table.py` | The audit table used by the job |

Check it worked:

```sql
SHOW SCHEMAS IN smart_factory_dev;
SHOW VOLUMES IN smart_factory_dev.raw;
```

